  "prompt": "When was the first remote control tv invented?",
  "task_completions": [
    "The first remote control for a television was invented in **1950** by Zenith Radio Corporation. It was called **\"Lazy Bone.\"**\n\nHere are a few key details about the early evolution of the TV remote:\n\n**1. The \"Lazy Bone\" (1950):**\nThis was a wired remote. It consisted of a cable connecting the remote control box to the television set. Because it was physically tethered to the TV, it didn't offer true wireless freedom, but it allowed the user to change channels without getting up.\n\n**2. The \"Flash-Matic\" (1955):**\nZenith released the first *wireless* remote in 1955. It worked using a flashlight-like beam. The user would point the remote at the TV, and photo cells in the corners of the television set would detect the light. Each channel had a different sensor location, so you had to aim the light at a specific part of the screen to change the channel.\n\n**3. The \"Space Command\" (1956):**\nThe Flash-Matic had a major flaw: sunlight hitting the TV could accidentally trigger a channel change. To fix this, Zenith invented the \"Space Command\" in 1956. This used **ultrasonic sound** (high-frequency clicks) produced by aluminum rods of different lengths inside the remote. This remained the industry standard for many years until infrared technology became common in the 1980s.",
    "The first remote control for a television was invented in **1950** by Zenith Radio Corporation. It was called **\"Lazy Bone.\"**\n\nHere are a few interesting details about its development and the versions that followed:\n\n### 1. The \"Lazy Bone\" (1950)\nThe Lazy Bone was a **wired** remote. It connected to the TV via a long cable, meaning you didn't have to get up to change the channel, but you were still physically tethered to the television set.\n\n### 2. The \"Flash-Matic\" (1955)\nBecause wires were cumbersome, Zenith released the **Flash-Matic** in 1955. This was the first **wireless** remote. It worked by shining a beam of light from a handheld flashlight onto photo-sensors in the corners of the TV screen. \n\n**The Flaw:** Because it relied on light, it was temperamental. If the sun shone through a window or a lamp was too bright, the TV would accidentally change channels on its own.\n\n### 3. The \"Space Command\" (1956)\nTo solve the light problem, Zenith introduced the **Space Command** in 1956. This was the first **ultrasonic** remote. It didn't use light; instead, it used high-frequency sound waves (similar to a dog whistle) that humans couldn't hear. When you pressed a button, a small hammer hit an aluminum rod inside the remote, creating a specific sound frequency that the TV recognized as a command.\n\nThis ultrasonic technology remained the standard for many years until **infrared (IR)** technology—the technology used in most remotes today—became common in the 1980s.",
    "The first remote control for a television was invented in **1950** by Zenith Radio Corporation. It was called the **\"Lazy Bone.\"**\n\nHere are a few key details about its development and the evolution of the technology:\n\n### 1. The \"Lazy Bone\" (1950)\nThe Lazy Bone was a **wired** remote. It consisted of a cable that ran from the TV set to a handheld device. Because it was physically connected, you didn't have to get up to change the channel, but you were still limited by the length of the cord.\n\n### 2. The \"Flash-Matic\" (1955)\nTo eliminate the cord, Zenith introduced the **Flash-Matic** in 1955. This was the first **wireless** remote. It worked by shining a beam of light from a handheld flashlight onto photo cells located in the corners of the TV screen. Depending on where you aimed the light, the TV would change the channel or turn off. \n\nThe biggest flaw was that sunlight hitting the TV could accidentally trigger the remote and change the channel.\n\n### 3. The \"Space Command\" (1956)\nTo solve the sunlight problem, Zenith released the **Space Command** in 1956. This was the first **ultrasonic** remote. Instead of light, it used high-frequency sound waves (above the range of human hearing). \n\nIt worked using a mechanical aluminum rod that, when pushed, struck a metal diaphragm to create a specific click/sound. Each button produced a different sound that the TV recognized as a specific command. This technology remained popular for decades until infrared (IR) remotes became the standard in the 1980s."
  ],
